<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  §3.1(1). Cleartext is off by default for targetSdk 28+, and this app speaks
  plain HTTP to a backend on a private mesh network. With no config the
  connection fails looking like a network error, not a policy one.

  The config wins over android:usesCleartextTraffic on API 24+, so the manifest
  flag is omitted entirely rather than set and silently ignored.

  Hostname route, per the brief's own preference. `ts.net` with subdomains
  covers every Tailscale MagicDNS name without the build needing to know which
  one, which is what makes this work for a host the user types at run time.
  `nord` does the same job for NordVPN Meshnet's own automatic per-device
  name (the `<something>.nord` "Nord Name" it assigns on pairing) - a second
  mesh product with the same shape: a name the user types at run time, not
  one the build can know in advance.

  What this deliberately does NOT cover: a bare 100.x.y.z or a 192.168.x.y
  typed as digits, regardless of which mesh product produced it - Tailscale
  and Meshnet both hand out addresses in the same CGNAT block. A network
  security config matches hostname strings and cannot express a CIDR range,
  so covering that block would mean listing four million literals. Pair by
  name instead - which also survives the address changing. The app says so
  when the host it is given cannot be reached in cleartext.

  Checked against Android's own code, 2026-09-27 (AOSP
  android.security.net.config.ApplicationConfig.getConfigForHostname): a
  <domain> matches the exact name, or - with includeSubdomains - a name that
  ends in "." plus it. Nothing else: no ranges, no wildcards, and no public
  way to change this list while the app runs. OkHttp asks
  NetworkSecurityPolicy.isCleartextTrafficPermitted(url.host) before every
  plain http:// connection, so this file is enforced on every request.

  Also left off ON PURPOSE (2026-09-27), although the app's own rule
  (OwnNetwork.kt, the backend's) counts them as the owner's own networks:
  `.local`, `.lan`, `.home.arpa` and other home-network names. Two reasons.
  (1) Jarvis on the PC listens only on its Tailscale/Meshnet address and on
  the PC itself - Jarvis Desktop refuses to save any other listening address
  (validate_bind_address, commands.rs) - so, set up the documented way, a
  home-network name, which points at the PC's Wi-Fi address, reaches
  nothing. (2) This phone moves
  between networks and keeps its link open in the background. A `.local`
  name is looked up by shouting the question to every device on whatever
  Wi-Fi the phone is on (multicast DNS), and any of them can answer it, so
  on a cafe's Wi-Fi the pairing key would go, unscrambled, to whichever
  stranger answered first. A .ts.net or .nord name is answered by the mesh
  app while it is on (the ordinary internet does not list them), and the
  traffic then travels inside the mesh's encryption; with the mesh off, the
  name normally fails to resolve and nothing is sent.
  docs/ARCHITECTURE.md section 2, "Which addresses the phone can use".

  The app's own check agrees with this list and refuses the rest up front,
  in plain words (data/PhoneAddress.kt), rather than accepting an address
  every request would then fail on. PhoneAddressTest reads THIS file and
  fails if the app's copy of the list differs from it.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />

    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">ts.net</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">nord</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">localhost</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
